# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
augustine-3.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: augustine-3.logic.r1 has "heart_unstable" and "strive_eternal" and "¬understand_wisdom"; augustine-3.logic.r1.2 and augustine-3.logic.r1.3 have "heart_unstable" and "strive_eternal"; they differ when only "their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come, and is still unstable", "they understand how the things be made, which by Thee, and in Thee are made" and "they strive to comprehend things eternal" are true, which the second allows
augustine-3.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps augustine-3.logic.r1, to: Suppose "their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come, and is still unstable", "they understand how the things be made, which by Thee, and in Thee are made" and "they strive to comprehend things eternal" are true. Is S1, "Who speak thus, do not yet understand Thee, O Wisdom of God, Light of souls, understand not yet how the things be made, which by Thee, and in Thee are made: yet they strive to comprehend things eternal, whilst their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come, and is still unstable.", true then?
augustine-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: augustine-3.logic.r1 has "eternal_whole_present ∧ ¬time_all_present → incomparable"; augustine-3.logic.r1.2 has "eternal_whole_present ∧ ¬time_all_present ∧ long_from_motions → incomparable" and "heart_unstable ∧ incomparable → ¬understand_wisdom"; they differ when only "their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come, and is still unstable", "they understand how the things be made, which by Thee, and in Thee are made", "in the Eternal nothing passeth, but the whole is present", "some time is all at once present", "a long time cannot become long, but out of many motions passing by" and "eternity cannot be compared with the times which are never fixed" are true, which the first allows; augustine-3.logic.r1.3 has "eternal_whole_present ∧ ¬time_all_present ∧ long_from_motions → incomparable" and "strive_eternal ∧ heart_unstable → ¬understand_wisdom" [in 1 of 2 runs]
augustine-3.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: augustine-3.logic.r1 has "incomparable"; augustine-3.logic.r1.2 and augustine-3.logic.r1.3 have "¬understand_wisdom"; they differ when only "they understand how the things be made, which by Thee, and in Thee are made" and "eternity cannot be compared with the times which are never fixed" are true, which the first allows
augustine-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: augustine-3.logic.r1 puts it at S2, "Who shall hold it, and fix it, that it be settled awhile, and awhile catch the glory of that ever-fixed Eternity, and compare it with the times which are never fixed, and see that it cannot be compared; and that a long time cannot become long, but out of many motions passing by, which cannot be prolonged altogether; but that in the Eternal nothing passeth, but the whole is present; whereas no time is all at once present: and that all time past, is driven on by time to come, and all to come followeth upon the past; and all past and to come, is created, and flows out of that which is ever present?"; augustine-3.logic.r1.2 and augustine-3.logic.r1.3 at S1, "Who speak thus, do not yet understand Thee, O Wisdom of God, Light of souls, understand not yet how the things be made, which by Thee, and in Thee are made: yet they strive to comprehend things eternal, whilst their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come, and is still unstable." [in 1 of 2 runs]
augustine-3.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps augustine-3.logic.r1.2 and augustine-3.logic.r1.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S2, "Who shall hold it, and fix it, that it be settled awhile, and awhile catch the glory of that ever-fixed Eternity, and compare it with the times which are never fixed, and see that it cannot be compared; and that a long time cannot become long, but out of many motions passing by, which cannot be prolonged altogether; but that in the Eternal nothing passeth, but the whole is present; whereas no time is all at once present: and that all time past, is driven on by time to come, and all to come followeth upon the past; and all past and to come, is created, and flows out of that which is ever present?", rather than S1, "Who speak thus, do not yet understand Thee, O Wisdom of God, Light of souls, understand not yet how the things be made, which by Thee, and in Thee are made: yet they strive to comprehend things eternal, whilst their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come, and is still unstable."? [in 1 of 2 runs]
augustine-3.md:1: argue/negation: augustine-3.logic.r1 P7 (S2): the sentence says "nothing", but P7 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "all past and to come is created, and flows out of that which is ever present" [in 1 of 2 runs]
